Right, and we think that we're getting people more, paying attention more if we present the problem as really, really big, that'll get their attention and maybe they'll finally stop doing what they're doing. But in fact, the scale of the problem being so big is what causes an efficacy, which then turns into less action. For some people, Sarah says, scare tactics don't produce apathy, but what she calls a martyrdom complex.

